Subject: a journal that replays a superblock is not to be believed

jbd2 replays whatever the log holds, by block number, and block 1 of a
1 KiB volume is the superblock. Patch 0023 gave that branch a writer;
nothing gave it a reader that asks whether what landed is still a
filesystem.

The reload after replay -- added by 0023, and correct, because the flag
edits and orphan cleanup that follow must not run on a stale superblock
-- loads whatever is on the medium. A log staging arbitrary bytes for
block 1 is four debugfs commands to produce, and it is also what a torn
write into a journal can leave. The very next thing the code does is
shift by s_log_block_size:

    Core/lwext4/include/ext4_super.h:95:14: runtime error: shift exponent
    955747801 is too large for 32-bit type 'int'

and the flag edits below would then write that superblock back out over
the medium, so a volume that recovery could not fix would come back with
RECOVER cleared and no record that anything went wrong.

Two changes, at two levels:

* jbd_recover checks the superblock it just reloaded -- magic, and a
  block-size shift ext4 can actually produce -- and fails with EIO when
  it does not. Recovery cannot repair this and must not paper over it:
  EXT4_FINCOM_RECOVER stays set, and e2fsck gets the volume.

* ext4_sb_get_block_size clamps the shift. It is an inline accessor with
  no way to report an error, and every caller in the tree shifts by
  whatever it returns, so undefined behaviour there is reachable from
  anywhere a superblock has been read. The callers that matter check the
  superblock themselves; this is so that the ones that do not cannot
  invoke UB while they are getting there.

Found by fuzzing, in the read-write pass, on the seed built to carry an
unreplayed journal -- the seed recipe itself was staging /dev/urandom for
blocks 1, 2 and 3. That recipe is fixed (the seed now stages inode-table
blocks with their own contents, so replay is a no-op that still has to be
performed) and the hostile version is kept as a fixture.

Fixture Tests/fixtures/hostile/0005-a-journal-that-replays-the-superblock.img.gz.

--- a/include/ext4_super.h
+++ b/include/ext4_super.h
@@ -92,7 +92,19 @@
  * @return  block size in bytes*/
 static inline uint32_t ext4_sb_get_block_size(struct ext4_sblock *s)
 {
-	return 1024 << to_le32(s->log_block_size);
+	uint32_t log = to_le32(s->log_block_size);
+
+	/* ext4 tops out at 64 KiB blocks, so anything above 6 is corruption --
+	 * and shifting by it is undefined, not merely wrong. This is not a
+	 * theoretical guard: a journal that replayed arbitrary bytes over the
+	 * superblock left 955747801 here, and every caller of this function
+	 * then shifted by it. Clamped rather than refused because this is an
+	 * inline accessor with no way to report; the callers that matter check
+	 * the superblock themselves. */
+	if (log > 6)
+		log = 6;
+
+	return 1024u << log;
 }
 
